/* AWS track — open-ended drills, hazard questions, read-the-output items and
   recall. Answer the drills out loud before expanding; the hazard questions
   are the AI-verification set, where a plausible suggestion is the wrong one. */
window.LX = window.LX || { commands: [], scenarios: [], drills: [] };
LX.drills = LX.drills || [];
LX.quiz = LX.quiz || [];
LX.dangerQs = LX.dangerQs || [];
LX.outputQs = LX.outputQs || [];

/* ── Drills ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
LX.drills.push(

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'Give me the IAM policy evaluation order, and say what each layer can and cannot do.',
  a:'An explicit Deny anywhere ends it immediately — no later layer can override it. Then Service Control Policies must allow the action; they only ever restrict, they never grant, and they are invisible from inside a member account. Then a resource-based policy is checked — and within a single account an allow there is sufficient on its own, without the identity policy also allowing. Then the identity policy must allow it. Then a permissions boundary, if one is attached, must also allow it. Then any session policy passed at assume-role time must also allow it. Everything is denied by default, so "no statement matched" produces exactly the same outcome as a deliberate denial.',
  points:['Explicit deny wins, always, at any layer',
    'SCPs bound; they never grant, and no account administrator can override one',
    'Boundaries and session policies are intersections — attaching more policy under them changes nothing',
    'Same-account: a resource policy allow is enough on its own. Cross-account: both sides must allow'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'A role has AdministratorAccess and still gets AccessDenied. Name every possible cause.',
  a:'An SCP on the account or an OU above it. A permissions boundary on the role capping it below admin. A session policy passed when the credential was minted. An explicit Deny in an inline policy on the role itself. A resource policy that denies — a bucket policy with a SecureTransport or SourceVpce condition, or a KMS key policy that simply does not name the principal. Or the caller is not the role you think, which `sts get-caller-identity` settles in one call. The error message distinguishes several of these, so reading it in full is the fastest route.',
  points:['SCP is the one an account administrator cannot see or override',
    'Boundary and session policy are ceilings, not grants',
    'KMS key policies are mandatory — an identity policy alone never grants key use',
    'Always confirm the identity before reading any policy'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'What is the difference between a role\'s trust policy and its permissions policy?',
  a:'The trust policy answers "who may become this role" and lives on the role as `AssumeRolePolicyDocument`. The permissions policies answer "what can it then do" and are attached or inline. A failure on `sts:AssumeRole` is always the first — and it needs both an allow in the caller\'s identity policy and a matching principal in the trust policy, so a cross-account assume requires cooperation from both accounts. An AccessDenied on any other API call is always the second.',
  points:['Two policies, two questions, two different failures',
    'Cross-account assume needs both sides: caller identity policy and target trust policy',
    'A trust on account:root trusts anything in that account with sts:AssumeRole permission',
    'External IDs exist to stop the confused-deputy problem with third parties'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'vpc', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'What actually makes a subnet public?',
  a:'A route table entry sending 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway. That is the whole definition — there is no "public" flag on a subnet. An instance in it also needs a public or Elastic IP to be reachable from outside, and the security group and NACL have to allow the traffic. A subnet with no explicit route table association silently inherits the VPC main table, which is how a newly created subnet behaves completely differently from its neighbours while looking identical.',
  points:['0.0.0.0/0 → internet gateway, in the route table associated with that subnet',
    'A public IP on the instance is a separate requirement',
    'Unassociated subnets follow the main route table — a very common surprise',
    'MapPublicIpOnLaunch decides whether new instances get an address automatically'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'sg', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures','aws-sec:infrastructure-security'],
  q:'Security groups versus network ACLs. When does the difference actually matter?',
  a:'Security groups are stateful, allow-only, and attach to network interfaces; NACLs are stateless, ordered, allow and deny, and apply to a whole subnet. The difference matters when traffic works one way only: because a NACL is stateless, the response to an allowed inbound connection needs its own outbound rule to the client\'s ephemeral port range, 1024–65535. A NACL allowing inbound 443 and outbound 443 accepts requests and drops every reply, producing a timeout that looks exactly like a dead service. It also matters when you need a deny — security groups cannot express one at all.',
  points:['Stateful versus stateless is the whole practical difference',
    'Ephemeral ports 1024–65535 on the return path for NACLs',
    'NACL rules are evaluated lowest number first and the first match wins',
    'Security groups can reference other security groups; NACLs only understand CIDRs'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'vpc', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:cost-optimized'],
  q:'An instance in a private subnet cannot reach the internet. Walk me through the causes.',
  a:'No default route in the subnet\'s route table, or one whose state is blackhole because the NAT gateway it pointed at was recreated with a new id. A NAT gateway placed in a private subnet, so it has nowhere to forward to. The NAT gateway\'s own subnet missing its route to the internet gateway — two route tables are involved and people check only one. A stateless NACL allowing outbound 443 but not inbound ephemeral ports. Or the destination is an AWS service that should be going through a VPC endpoint, where an interface endpoint without private DNS enabled exists and is used by nobody.',
  points:['Private subnet → NAT gateway, and the NAT\'s subnet → internet gateway',
    'blackhole in a route table is silent and total',
    'A NAT gateway is zonal: one for three AZs is a cost and an availability problem',
    'S3 and DynamoDB gateway endpoints are free and usually the biggest NAT saving'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'s3', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:data-protection'],
  q:'A role can list a bucket but cannot read objects. What is wrong, and what else produces a 403?',
  a:'Almost certainly the policy names `arn:aws:s3:::bucket` but not `arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*`. ListBucket acts on the bucket, GetObject acts on the objects, and they are different resources. Beyond that: a Deny in the bucket policy, often conditional on aws:SecureTransport, encryption headers or aws:SourceVpce; a KMS key policy that does not name the principal, which S3 reports as a plain AccessDenied with no mention of KMS; Block Public Access neutralising a policy that grants public access; object ownership and ACLs after a cross-account upload; and a VPC endpoint policy scoped to other buckets.',
  points:['Bucket ARN for bucket-level actions, bucket/* for object-level',
    'An S3 403 with no obvious cause is very often KMS',
    'Conditional denies in bucket policies are where the surprises live',
    'Endpoint policies are invisible from the caller\'s side'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'kms', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:data-protection'],
  q:'Why is a KMS key policy different from every other resource policy in AWS?',
  a:'Because it is mandatory rather than additive. For most resources an identity policy alone is enough within the same account; for KMS the key policy must also allow the principal, either directly or by delegating to IAM with a statement allowing the account root. That is why cross-account KMS access needs the key policy to name the principal *and* the caller\'s identity policy to allow the KMS action. AWS-managed keys cannot have their policy edited at all, which is the practical reason to create a customer-managed key. Grants are the third mechanism: created programmatically by services like EBS and RDS, invisible in the key policy, and easy to miss in a review.',
  points:['Key policy is required, not optional — identity policy alone grants nothing',
    'Cross-account needs both sides, and a customer-managed key',
    'Grants are real permissions that do not appear in the key policy',
    'AWS-managed keys are uneditable, so they cannot be shared'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sysops:reliability'],
  q:'An Auto Scaling group is replacing instances in a loop. What are the candidates?',
  a:'A health check grace period shorter than the application\'s boot time, so every instance is killed just before it becomes healthy. A health check type of EC2 when an ELB check is what you want, or the reverse — a failing target group taking healthy instances out. A user-data script that fails, commonly because the private subnet cannot reach the package repository. Or launches that never succeed at all, from a vCPU quota, insufficient capacity for that type in that AZ, or an exhausted subnet — those show as Failed activities and never reach a health check. `describe-scaling-activities` names which one in plain English.',
  points:['Grace period shorter than boot time is the classic loop',
    'A failed launch never gets to health checking — read StatusCode first',
    'Suspend ReplaceUnhealthy to get a live instance to inspect',
    'The Cause field is a sentence and it is usually the answer'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-saa:high-performing'],
  q:'An ALB returns 502 for some requests and 503 for others. What does each mean?',
  a:'503 means the load balancer had no healthy target to route to — the target group is empty or everything is failing its health check. 502 means it did reach a target and could not use the response: a connection reset, a malformed response, or a timeout mid-request. `describe-target-health` splits it further through the Reason field: Target.Timeout is a security group or nothing listening, Target.FailedHealthChecks is the check reaching the app and disagreeing with it, and Target.NotRegistered means the scaling group never added the instance.',
  points:['503 is no healthy targets; 502 is a bad response from one',
    'Read TargetHealth.Reason rather than State',
    'target_processing_time at exactly the idle timeout is a keep-alive mismatch',
    'A health check that queries the database fails the whole fleet at once'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'rds', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:resilient-architectures'],
  q:'Is Multi-AZ a scaling feature? What does a failover actually do to your application?',
  a:'No — the standby serves no traffic and exists only for availability. A failover promotes the standby and updates the DNS record for the endpoint, taking a minute or two, and the application must reconnect. Connection pools holding open sockets across a failover fail in exactly that shape: a burst of errors that looks like an application bug. The fixes are client-side — connect by endpoint name and never cache the IP, keep connection lifetimes short, set aggressive socket timeouts, and retry idempotent operations. Read scaling is read replicas, which are asynchronous, so a read straight after a write can legitimately miss it.',
  points:['Multi-AZ is availability; read replicas are scaling',
    'Failover is a DNS change — caching an IP address guarantees an outage',
    'describe-events shows failovers as a pair a minute apart',
    'Test it by triggering a failover deliberately, not by hoping'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:logging-and-monitoring'],
  q:'Something changed and nobody owns it. How do you find out who and what?',
  a:'CloudTrail for who called what and when — searchable by resource name, event name or username. Then read the full event rather than the summary: userIdentity distinguishes a user from an assumed role, sessionContext names the role, the session name often carries the human or the pipeline run, and the user agent tells you whether this was Terraform or the console. Then AWS Config for what the resource looked like before and after, which is the diff CloudTrail cannot give you. Then drift detection, because a console change on top of an IaC-managed resource silently reverts at the next apply.',
  points:['CloudTrail is who and when; Config is before and after',
    'lookup-events covers ninety days and management events only',
    'Data events are off by default — enable them before you need them',
    'role-session-name should be a person or a ticket, so this question is answerable'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:logging-and-monitoring'],
  q:'What are CloudTrail\'s blind spots, and how do you cover them?',
  a:'Three. `lookup-events` only reaches back ninety days — for anything older you query the trail\'s S3 bucket, usually with Athena. It covers management events only; data events such as S3 GetObject, KMS Decrypt and Lambda Invoke are off by default and cost extra, and they are exactly what you need to answer "was the data actually read". And a single-region trail misses activity elsewhere, which is where a compromised credential often starts. The coverage worth having is an organisation trail, multi-region, with log file validation on, delivering to a locked S3 bucket in a separate account.',
  points:['Ninety days through the API; the S3 bucket holds the rest',
    'Data events answer breach questions and are off by default',
    'Log file validation makes the record tamper-evident',
    'A trail in the same account it audits is a weak control'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'ec2', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-sysops:monitoring'],
  q:'What do the two EC2 status checks tell you, and what should you do about each?',
  a:'The system status check covers the host, its network and its power. If it fails, it is AWS\'s side: stop and start the instance to move it to different hardware — a reboot keeps it on the same host and achieves nothing. The instance status check covers your operating system: a failed boot, an exhausted root filesystem, a bad fstab entry, a kernel that will not load. No amount of stopping and starting fixes that, and the console output usually names it in the last twenty lines.',
  points:['System check = AWS hardware; stop/start migrates hosts, reboot does not',
    'Instance check = your OS; read get-console-output',
    'Both passing means the instance is fine and the path to it is not',
    'get-console-screenshot is the only way to read a Windows failure'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'ec2', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'Why prefer SSM Session Manager over SSH, and what has to be true for it to work?',
  a:'It removes port 22 from every security group, removes key distribution and rotation entirely, and puts every session in CloudTrail with the principal attached. Three things must be true: the SSM agent runs on the instance, the instance profile includes AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore, and the agent can reach the SSM endpoints — either through NAT or through the ssm, ssmmessages and ec2messages interface endpoints. The third is the usual cause of "the agent is installed but the instance is not managed", and it is also what makes a fully private subnet manageable.',
  points:['No inbound rule, no key pair, full audit trail',
    'Agent + instance role + endpoint reachability, all three',
    'Port forwarding reaches a private RDS endpoint without a bastion',
    'describe-instance-information shows which instances are actually reachable'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'dr', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:resilient-architectures'],
  q:'Someone asks whether your backups work. What do you check, and what does "work" mean?',
  a:'Coverage first: `backup list-protected-resources` shows what a plan actually covers, and anything not on it is unprotected regardless of intent. Then success: `list-backup-jobs --by-state FAILED` is the only standing check that matters. Then recoverability, which nothing but an actual restore proves — restore into a new instance, time it, and that number is your real RTO. Then blast radius: snapshots are regional, so a DR plan that never copies to a second region is a backup plan; and a vault lock in compliance mode is what makes recovery points survive a compromised administrator.',
  points:['Coverage, success, and a tested restore are three separate questions',
    'A restore always creates a new resource — it never overwrites, so start early',
    'RDS automated snapshots are deleted with the instance; take a final manual one',
    'Cross-region copy and vault lock are what separate DR from backup'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'cost', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:cost-optimized'],
  q:'Where does the money actually go in a typical VPC-heavy account, and what would you fix first?',
  a:'NAT gateway processing and cross-AZ data transfer are usually the surprising ones: every byte a private subnet sends to S3 through NAT is billed twice over when a free gateway endpoint would have carried it. Then unattached EBS volumes and old snapshots, which nobody ever deletes. Then Elastic IPs allocated to nothing, billed hourly. Then over-provisioned instances, which Compute Optimizer will name for you. The first fix is almost always the S3 and DynamoDB gateway endpoints, because they are free, take minutes, and remove traffic rather than shifting it.',
  points:['S3/DynamoDB gateway endpoints are free and usually the biggest single win',
    'One NAT gateway serving three AZs bills cross-AZ transfer on top of processing',
    'Detached volumes, orphaned snapshots and idle Elastic IPs are pure waste',
    'Deregistering an AMI does not delete its snapshots'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'org', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'What are Service Control Policies for, and what can they not do?',
  a:'They set the maximum permissions any principal in an account can have, and they are inherited down the OU tree cumulatively. They cannot grant anything — an SCP allowing everything grants nothing on its own — and they do not apply to the management account or to service-linked roles. Typical uses are denying regions outside an approved list, denying the disabling of CloudTrail, GuardDuty or Config, denying deletion of resources carrying a protection tag, and denying the creation of IAM users where SSO is the standard. The failure mode to understand is that they are invisible from inside a member account.',
  points:['A filter, never a grant; inherited cumulatively down the OU tree',
    'Do not apply to the management account — a real reason not to run workloads there',
    'The denial message says "explicit deny in a service control policy"',
    'Test in a sandbox OU first: an over-broad SCP can lock out an entire organisation'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'img', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sysops:deployment'],
  q:'You updated the AMI in a launch template and nothing changed. Why?',
  a:'Three separate things have to happen. Creating a new launch template version does not move the `$Default` pointer, so a group following `$Default` or a pinned version number ignores it. Even a group following `$Latest` only uses the new version for **new** launches. And existing instances are never replaced by a template change — that requires `start-instance-refresh` or a deliberate rolling replacement. Skipping the last step is how a fleet ends up running three different AMIs simultaneously with nothing indicating it.',
  points:['$Latest and $Default are different pointers and neither is automatic',
    'Template changes affect new launches only',
    'start-instance-refresh is what actually rolls the fleet',
    'Hard-coded AMI ids never signal that they are stale — use the SSM public parameter'] },

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'How would you get long-lived access keys off every laptop in a team?',
  a:'IAM Identity Center with `aws configure sso`, so the CLI gets a short-lived credential from a browser login and sessions expire on their own. Then delete the IAM users, or at minimum deactivate their keys — deactivation is instantly reversible, which makes it the safe first move when nobody is sure what a key is for. Use the credential report to find them all, including the ones belonging to people who left. For CI, replace keys with OIDC federation so GitHub Actions or GitLab assumes a role directly with no stored secret at all.',
  points:['Identity Center for humans, OIDC federation for pipelines',
    'Deactivate before deleting — reversible, and it surfaces the owner quickly',
    'The credential report finds every key, its age and its last use',
    'A key in a repository or a CI variable is the most common route to a real compromise'] }

);

/* ── Hazard questions ───────────────────────────────────────── */
LX.dangerQs.push(

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'A deploy role is getting AccessDenied. An assistant offers these. Which would you never do?',
  choices:['Attach AdministratorAccess to unblock the deploy and tighten it later',
    'Run simulate-principal-policy against the exact action and resource ARN',
    'Read the full error text to see which layer denied it',
    'Check whether an SCP or a permissions boundary applies'],
  a:0,
  why:'"Tighten it later" is the phrase that never happens, and it usually does not even work: if the denial came from an SCP, a boundary or an explicit deny, more permission changes nothing — so you end up with an over-privileged role *and* the original outage. The role also now has standing access to everything in the account, which is a finding the moment anyone looks.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'sg', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-sec:infrastructure-security'],
  q:'You cannot SSH to an instance. Which of these is the dangerous option?',
  choices:['Add 0.0.0.0/0 on port 22 to the security group to rule it out',
    'Check whether the NACL allows return traffic on ephemeral ports',
    'Use SSM Session Manager, which needs no inbound rule at all',
    'Read the flow logs to see whether packets arrive at all'],
  a:0,
  why:'Opening SSH to the entire internet exposes the instance to continuous automated attack within minutes, and the "temporary" rule almost always survives the incident. If the NACL was the real problem it does not even help. Session Manager answers the same question with no exposure, and flow logs tell you whether packets arrive without changing anything.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'s3', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-sec:data-protection'],
  q:'A job cannot read from a bucket. Which suggestion is the one to refuse?',
  choices:['Turn off Block Public Access and make the objects public to unblock it',
    'Add the bucket/* ARN form to the role policy',
    'Check whether the objects are encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key',
    'Read the bucket policy for a conditional Deny'],
  a:0,
  why:'Making a bucket public to solve a permissions problem is the incident everyone can name, and it resolves nothing that a scoped policy would not. Block Public Access exists precisely to stop this being possible in a moment of pressure. If a specific object genuinely needs to be shared outside AWS, a presigned URL carries your permissions for a bounded time instead.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'A user is locked out and cannot sign in. Which action would you not take?',
  choices:['Disable MFA on their account so they can get in, and re-enable it afterwards',
    'Check CloudTrail for the failed ConsoleLogin events and their source IP',
    'Have them re-register a new MFA device through the documented recovery process',
    'Verify their identity out of band before changing anything on the account'],
  a:0,
  why:'Removing MFA is exactly what an attacker with a stolen password needs, and lockout complaints are a standard social-engineering opening. Re-enabling it "afterwards" depends on someone remembering. The recovery path should be a documented process with out-of-band identity verification, and the failed sign-in records tell you whether this is a genuine lockout or an attempt in progress.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'ec2', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sysops:reliability'],
  q:'An instance is unreachable and its instance status check is failing. Which is the destructive option?',
  choices:['Terminate it and let Auto Scaling replace it, before reading anything',
    'Read get-console-output to see how far it got in booting',
    'Attach its root volume to another instance and inspect the filesystem',
    'Check whether the root volume is full'],
  a:0,
  why:'Termination destroys the only copy of the evidence, and if the cause is in the AMI or the user data the replacement fails the same way — now with nothing left to examine. The console output costs one call and usually names the failure outright. Replacing is often the right *eventual* action; doing it before capturing anything means the next occurrence starts from zero.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'rds', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:resilient-architectures'],
  q:'Testing connectivity to a database. Which of these is the one to avoid?',
  choices:['Set PubliclyAccessible to true so you can connect from your laptop',
    'Use SSM port forwarding through an instance in the same VPC',
    'Run nc from an instance that actually shares the application\'s network path',
    'Check the security group allows the application\'s group on the database port'],
  a:0,
  why:'It exposes the database to the internet, it proves nothing about the application\'s actual path, and the setting outlives the incident — this is a standing audit finding and a real breach route. Testing from where the application runs is both safer and more informative, and SSM port forwarding gives you a local connection with no exposure at all.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:logging-and-monitoring'],
  q:'An alarm is noisy and waking people up. Which response is the wrong one?',
  choices:['Delete the alarm, since nobody trusts it any more',
    'Look at the alarm history to see whether it is flapping around a threshold',
    'Set an evaluation period so it needs several breaches before firing',
    'Check whether TreatMissingData is turning gaps into alarms'],
  a:0,
  why:'Deleting it removes the coverage while leaving whatever it was watching unwatched, and nobody revisits the decision. A flapping alarm is usually a threshold or evaluation-period problem, and TreatMissingData set to breaching turns every metric gap into a page. Tune it or replace it deliberately — but an alarm that is deleted during an incident is never recreated.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'org', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'You are writing an SCP to block a risky action. Which approach is dangerous?',
  choices:['Attach a Deny "*" SCP at the organisation root and refine it from there',
    'Test it on a sandbox OU containing one throwaway account first',
    'Write it as a Deny with a narrow condition rather than a broad NotAction',
    'Confirm the management account is unaffected before attaching anything'],
  a:0,
  why:'An over-broad SCP at the root applies to every account immediately and can lock out the very roles needed to remove it — including automation and break-glass paths. SCPs do not apply to the management account, which is the escape hatch, but relying on that under pressure is not a plan. Test on a sandbox OU, and keep the blast radius of the first attachment small.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'kms', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:data-protection'],
  q:'A service is failing with a KMS error. Which action is irreversible in the bad way?',
  choices:['Schedule the key for deletion to "clean up" a key you think is unused',
    'Read the key policy to see which principals it names',
    'List grants on the key, since services create permissions that are not in the policy',
    'Check whether the caller has kms:Decrypt in its identity policy as well'],
  a:0,
  why:'Deleting a KMS key permanently destroys every piece of data encrypted with it — snapshots, S3 objects, secrets, RDS storage — and no support case recovers it. The minimum waiting period exists for exactly this reason. Disable the key instead: it produces the same failures, proves whether anything still needs it, and is instantly reversible.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'dr', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:resilient-architectures'],
  q:'Restoring a database after a bad migration. Which is the risky choice?',
  choices:['Restore over the existing instance to keep the same endpoint',
    'Restore to a new instance and repoint the application once it is verified',
    'Check LatestRestorableTime before choosing a restore point',
    'Take a manual snapshot of the current state before doing anything'],
  a:0,
  why:'RDS restores always create a new instance — but the instinct behind this option leads people to delete the original first to free the name, which destroys the current state before the restore is verified. Restore beside it, verify, then cut over. A manual snapshot of the broken state costs almost nothing and preserves the ability to investigate what the migration actually did.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sysops:reliability'],
  q:'An Auto Scaling group is churning. Which suggestion makes it worse?',
  choices:['Raise desired capacity so enough instances survive to serve traffic',
    'Suspend ReplaceUnhealthy so a failing instance stays alive to inspect',
    'Read the scaling activities to see the Cause the group recorded',
    'Compare the health check grace period against measured boot time'],
  a:0,
  why:'It multiplies the failing launches, burns vCPU quota, may hit capacity limits in the AZ, and hides the loop behind more instances doing exactly the same thing. It also costs money for instances that never become healthy. The loop has a cause the group has already written down in plain English — read it first.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'vpc', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'A private subnet cannot reach the internet. Which fix is the wrong one?',
  choices:['Add a 0.0.0.0/0 route to the internet gateway in the private subnet',
    'Check whether the existing NAT route is in blackhole state',
    'Verify the NAT gateway sits in a subnet that has an internet gateway route',
    'Add a gateway endpoint if the destination is S3'],
  a:0,
  why:'That makes the subnet public. Anything in it with a public IP becomes internet-reachable, security groups become the only line of defence, and it usually does not even work — instances in a private subnet have no public address, so they still cannot reach anything. The blackhole route and the NAT gateway\'s own placement are the two causes that actually produce this symptom.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:logging-and-monitoring'],
  q:'During an investigation, which of these compromises the evidence?',
  choices:['Reverting the change immediately so the system is safe again',
    'Capturing the CloudTrail event and the Config item before touching anything',
    'Taking a snapshot of the affected volume for offline analysis',
    'Noting the session name and source IP from the full event record'],
  a:0,
  why:'Reverting first is a genuine trade — safety against evidence — and there are incidents where you must. But done reflexively it destroys the record of what changed, and if the change came from automation it simply comes back. Capture is seconds of work: the CloudTrail event, the Config configuration item, and a volume snapshot cost almost nothing and are unrecoverable once gone.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'Cleaning up IAM after an audit. Which action would you not take unattended?',
  choices:['Delete every access key that has not been used in ninety days, in one pass',
    'Deactivate them first and wait for something to break or someone to speak up',
    'Cross-reference the credential report against CloudTrail before touching anything',
    'Start with keys belonging to users who have left the company'],
  a:0,
  why:'Deletion is irreversible and last-used data has real gaps — a key used by a quarterly job or a disaster-recovery path looks abandoned right up until the day it matters. Deactivation produces exactly the same failures, surfaces the owner within a cycle, and is reversible in one call. Delete after a deliberate waiting period, not in the same pass as the discovery.' }

);

/* ── Read the output ────────────────────────────────────────── */
LX.outputQs.push(

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'intermediate', cmd:'aws sts get-caller-identity --query Arn --output text',
  out:'arn:aws:sts::111122223333:assumed-role/ci-deploy/i-0a1b2c3d4e5f',
  q:'Whose policy should you be reading?',
  choices:['The ci-deploy role\'s','The IAM user who created the instance','The instance profile\'s trust policy only','The account root policy'],
  a:0, why:'An assumed-role ARN names the role and the session. Permissions come from the role `ci-deploy`, not from whoever created the instance or the profile. The session part after the role name is just an identifier — on an instance it is the instance id.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'vpc', level:'beginner', cmd:'aws ec2 describe-route-tables --query \'RouteTables[].Routes[]\'',
  out:'DestinationCidrBlock   GatewayId   NatGatewayId    State\n10.0.0.0/16            local       None            active\n0.0.0.0/0              None        nat-07c9e1      blackhole',
  q:'What is happening to outbound traffic?',
  choices:['It is silently dropped — the NAT gateway no longer exists',
    'It is routed to the internet gateway instead',
    'It is being denied by a NACL',
    'Nothing is wrong; blackhole means idle'],
  a:0, why:'`blackhole` means the next hop is gone — usually a NAT gateway that was recreated with a new id while the route kept pointing at the old one. Every matching packet is discarded with no error anywhere, so the instance just reports a timeout.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'intermediate', cmd:'aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn $TG',
  out:'i-0a1b2c3d   unhealthy   Target.Timeout             Request timed out\ni-0c4d5e6f   unhealthy   Target.FailedHealthChecks  Health checks failed with code 302',
  q:'What are these two targets telling you?',
  choices:['One is unreachable on the check port; the other answers with a redirect',
    'Both are overloaded',
    'Both failed to register with the target group',
    'The load balancer itself is broken'],
  a:0, why:'Target.Timeout means nothing answered — a security group or nothing listening on that port. Target.FailedHealthChecks with a 302 means the check reached the app and got a redirect where the matcher wanted 200. Two different problems in one table, and the State column shows neither.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'s3', level:'intermediate', cmd:'aws s3api head-object --bucket prod-data --key reports/q3.csv',
  out:'{\n    "ServerSideEncryption": "aws:kms",\n    "SSEKMSKeyId": "arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:111122223333:key/8f3b2a1c"\n}',
  q:'The role has s3:GetObject and still gets 403. What is the most likely cause?',
  choices:['The KMS key policy does not allow the principal',
    'The object does not exist',
    'Block Public Access is enabled',
    'The bucket is in another region'],
  a:0, why:'SSE-KMS with a customer-managed key needs `kms:Decrypt` and the key policy must name the principal — key policies are mandatory, not optional. S3 reports the failure as a plain AccessDenied with no mention of KMS, which is why this one is missed so often.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'sg', level:'intermediate', cmd:'aws ec2 describe-network-acls --query \'NetworkAcls[].Entries[]\'',
  out:'RuleNumber  Egress  CidrBlock   RuleAction  PortRange\n100         False   0.0.0.0/0   allow       443 to 443\n32767       False   0.0.0.0/0   deny\n100         True    0.0.0.0/0   allow       443 to 443\n32767       True    0.0.0.0/0   deny',
  q:'An instance in this subnet makes an outbound HTTPS request. What happens?',
  choices:['The request goes out and the response is dropped',
    'Both directions work normally',
    'The request is blocked immediately',
    'Only the TLS handshake fails'],
  a:0, why:'NACLs are stateless. The request leaves on 443, but the response arrives on the client\'s ephemeral port — somewhere in 1024–65535 — which matches no allow rule and hits the default deny. The download hangs rather than failing cleanly, which disguises it as an application problem.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'rds', level:'intermediate', cmd:'aws rds describe-events --source-identifier prod-db --duration 1440',
  out:'2026-08-27T11:18:02Z   Multi-AZ instance failover started\n2026-08-27T11:19:14Z   Multi-AZ instance failover completed',
  q:'The application logged connection errors at 11:18. What is the fix?',
  choices:['Client-side: shorter connection lifetimes, socket timeouts and retries',
    'Scale the database up',
    'Disable Multi-AZ so failovers cannot happen',
    'Add a read replica'],
  a:0, why:'The failover worked exactly as designed and took seventy-two seconds. The errors came from a connection pool holding sockets to an endpoint that had moved. The database needs no change; the client needs to reconnect on failure and not cache the address.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'advanced', cmd:'aws cloudtrail lookup-events … --query \'Events[].CloudTrailEvent\' | jq .userIdentity',
  out:'{\n  "type": "AssumedRole",\n  "arn": "arn:aws:sts::111122223333:assumed-role/ci-deploy/GitHubActions-4821",\n  "sessionContext": { "sessionIssuer": { "userName": "ci-deploy" } }\n}',
  q:'Who made this change?',
  choices:['A pipeline run — GitHub Actions run 4821, via the ci-deploy role',
    'A user called GitHubActions',
    'The AWS service ci-deploy',
    'It cannot be determined'],
  a:0, why:'The session name carries the pipeline run and the sessionIssuer names the role that was assumed. The investigation moves to that run and the commit behind it — which is precisely why role-session-name should be a person or a ticket rather than "session1".' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'intermediate', cmd:'aws autoscaling describe-scaling-activities --auto-scaling-group-name app-prod',
  out:'Failed   You have requested more vCPU capacity than your current vCPU limit of 64\n         allows for the instance bucket that the specified instance type belongs\n         to. (VcpuLimitExceeded)',
  q:'Why are the instances never becoming healthy?',
  choices:['They are never launching at all — this is a quota limit',
    'The health check grace period is too short',
    'The AMI is broken',
    'The target group is rejecting them'],
  a:0, why:'A Failed activity means the launch itself did not happen, so nothing ever reached a health check. EC2 limits are counted in vCPUs per family group per region, not in instances, and only a quota increase resolves it.' }

);

/* ── Recall ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
LX.quiz.push(

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'Which of these wins over everything else in IAM evaluation?',
  choices:['An explicit Deny, at any layer','An identity policy Allow','A resource policy Allow','A permissions boundary Allow'],
  a:0, why:'An explicit Deny ends evaluation immediately and no later Allow can override it. Everything else is a question of whether enough layers allowed the action.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'iam', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'What does a permissions boundary do?',
  choices:['Caps what a role can do, regardless of what is attached to it',
    'Grants additional permissions to a role',
    'Restricts which principals may assume the role',
    'Limits how long a session lasts'],
  a:0, why:'A boundary is a ceiling and the effective permission set is the intersection with the attached policies — so attaching AdministratorAccess to a bounded role changes nothing. Who may assume the role is the trust policy; session length is MaxSessionDuration.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'org', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:identity-and-access'],
  q:'Can a Service Control Policy grant permissions?',
  choices:['No — it only ever restricts what identity policies can grant',
    'Yes, to any principal in the account','Yes, but only to roles','Yes, if FullAWSAccess is detached'],
  a:0, why:'SCPs are a filter on the maximum available permissions. FullAWSAccess attached alongside grants nothing on its own; permissions still have to come from an identity or resource policy inside the account.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'vpc', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'What makes a subnet public?',
  choices:['A route table entry sending 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway',
    'The MapPublicIpOnLaunch setting','A public NACL','Being in the default VPC'],
  a:0, why:'That route is the entire definition. MapPublicIpOnLaunch decides whether new instances get a public address, which is a separate requirement for reachability from outside.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'sg', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'Which statement about security groups is true?',
  choices:['They are stateful, so an allowed inbound request gets its reply out automatically',
    'They evaluate rules in order and stop at the first match',
    'They can contain deny rules','They apply to an entire subnet'],
  a:0, why:'Security groups are stateful and allow-only, with no rule ordering. Ordered rules, deny rules and subnet scope all describe NACLs instead.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'sg', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:infrastructure-security'],
  q:'A NACL allows inbound 443 and outbound 443. What breaks?',
  choices:['Responses to inbound requests, which return on ephemeral ports',
    'Nothing — that is a complete rule pair',
    'Only IPv6 traffic','The TLS handshake only'],
  a:0, why:'NACLs are stateless. Responses go to the client\'s ephemeral port in 1024–65535, so without an outbound rule covering that range every reply is dropped and the connection times out.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'s3', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:data-protection'],
  q:'Which ARN does s3:GetObject need?',
  choices:['arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*','arn:aws:s3:::bucket','Both, in the same statement','Neither — it uses the bucket name'],
  a:0, why:'Object actions act on the objects, so they need the /* form. ListBucket is a bucket-level action and needs the bare bucket ARN — which is why a policy with only one of them lets you list and not read.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'kms', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sec:data-protection'],
  q:'A role has kms:Decrypt in its identity policy but the key policy does not name it. What happens?',
  choices:['Denied — the key policy must allow it too',
    'Allowed — the identity policy is sufficient',
    'Allowed only within the same account',
    'Allowed only for AWS-managed keys'],
  a:0, why:'KMS key policies are mandatory rather than additive. The key policy must allow the principal directly, or delegate to IAM by allowing the account root. This is why an unexplained S3 403 is so often a KMS problem.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'ec2', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-sysops:monitoring'],
  q:'The system status check fails but the instance status check passes. What do you do?',
  choices:['Stop and start the instance to move it to different hardware',
    'Reboot the instance','Read the OS logs','Resize the root volume'],
  a:0, why:'A failed system check is the underlying host. A stop/start places the instance on new hardware; a reboot keeps it on the same host and changes nothing. The OS is fine — its own check passed.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'ec2', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'What does SSM Session Manager require on the instance side?',
  choices:['The agent, an instance role with SSM permissions, and reachable SSM endpoints',
    'An inbound rule on port 22','A public IP address','A key pair'],
  a:0, why:'No inbound rule, no key pair and no public address are needed. Endpoint reachability — through NAT or through ssm, ssmmessages and ec2messages interface endpoints — is the requirement people miss.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'beginner', cert:['aws-saa:high-performing'],
  q:'An ALB returns 503. What does that mean?',
  choices:['There are no healthy targets to route to',
    'A target returned an invalid response','TLS negotiation failed','The listener rule did not match'],
  a:0, why:'503 is no healthy targets. 502 is a target that was reached and returned something unusable. A listener rule that matches nothing produces a 404 from the default action instead.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'scale', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sysops:reliability'],
  q:'An ASG kills every instance about sixty seconds after launch. First thing to check?',
  choices:['The health check grace period against the application\'s boot time',
    'The maximum size of the group','The instance type','The subnet CIDR'],
  a:0, why:'A grace period shorter than boot time kills each instance just before it becomes healthy, forever. The scaling activities record the cause in plain English, which confirms it in one call.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'rds', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:resilient-architectures'],
  q:'What does RDS Multi-AZ provide?',
  choices:['Automatic failover to a standby that serves no traffic',
    'Read scaling across two instances','Cross-region disaster recovery','Higher IOPS'],
  a:0, why:'Multi-AZ is availability only — the standby is idle until failover, which is a DNS change taking a minute or two. Read scaling is read replicas, and cross-region DR needs cross-region replicas or copied snapshots.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'rds', level:'advanced', cert:['aws-sysops:reliability'],
  q:'You scale an RDS instance down a class and connections start failing. Why?',
  choices:['max_connections is derived from instance memory by default',
    'The endpoint changed','The security group was reset','Multi-AZ was disabled'],
  a:0, why:'The default parameter is a formula over DBInstanceClassMemory, so a smaller instance silently lowers the connection ceiling. Nothing in the application changed; the limit moved underneath it.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:logging-and-monitoring'],
  q:'How far back does cloudtrail lookup-events reach?',
  choices:['Ninety days, management events only','One year, all events','Thirty days, all events','Indefinitely'],
  a:0, why:'Ninety days and management events only. Data events such as GetObject and Decrypt are off by default and cost extra, and anything older lives in the trail\'s S3 bucket — usually queried with Athena.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'obs', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sec:logging-and-monitoring'],
  q:'Which service tells you what a resource looked like before a change?',
  choices:['AWS Config','CloudTrail','CloudWatch Logs','GuardDuty'],
  a:0, why:'Config records configuration items over time, so you can diff before and after. CloudTrail records that an API call happened and by whom, which is a different half of the same question.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'img', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-sysops:deployment'],
  q:'You create a new launch template version with a new AMI. What happens to the running fleet?',
  choices:['Nothing until an instance refresh or a new launch',
    'It rolls automatically','It reboots into the new AMI','The ASG scales to zero and back'],
  a:0, why:'Template changes affect new launches only, and only if the group follows $Latest or is repointed. Replacing the running instances requires start-instance-refresh or a deliberate rolling replacement.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'dr', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:resilient-architectures'],
  q:'What happens to automated RDS snapshots when the instance is deleted?',
  choices:['They are deleted with it unless a final manual snapshot is taken',
    'They are retained for the full retention period','They convert to manual snapshots','They move to the backup vault'],
  a:0, why:'Automated snapshots are tied to the instance lifecycle. A final manual snapshot at deletion is the difference between an accident and a disaster, and manual snapshots persist until you remove them.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'cost', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:cost-optimized'],
  q:'Which change most often produces the biggest NAT gateway saving?',
  choices:['Adding S3 and DynamoDB gateway endpoints',
    'Moving to a larger NAT gateway','Enabling flow logs','Adding a second internet gateway'],
  a:0, why:'Gateway endpoints are free and remove that traffic from NAT entirely rather than shifting it. NAT gateways do not have sizes, flow logs add cost, and a VPC can only have one internet gateway attached.' },

{ track:'aws', cat:'vpc', level:'intermediate', cert:['aws-saa:secure-architectures'],
  q:'An interface VPC endpoint exists but everything still goes out through NAT. Why?',
  choices:['PrivateDnsEnabled is false, so callers resolve the public endpoint',
    'The endpoint is in the wrong region','Gateway endpoints override it','The security group blocks it'],
  a:0, why:'Without private DNS, the service name resolves to its public address and the endpoint is never used — it runs, bills hourly and carries nothing. Enabling private DNS is what makes it transparent to callers.' }

);
